Safari has a couple of quirks that cause this. Usually it's one of these:

**1. Safari doesn't focus buttons on mouse click (macOS).** Clicking a `<button>` in Safari doesn't give it focus, so a `:focus` style never shows on click. That's expected browser behaviour, not your CSS. Keyboard focus is what matters for accessibility.

**2. Safari only tabs to buttons if a setting is on.** By default, Tab in Safari skips buttons and links. Test with **Option+Tab**, or turn on Safari > Settings > Advanced > "Press Tab to highlight each item on a webpage". Many "missing outline" reports are really this.

**3. A reset is removing it.** Look for `outline: none` / `outline: 0` on `button`, `*:focus` or `-webkit-appearance: none` in a reset or framework stylesheet. If you remove the default outline, add your own back.

A robust version:

```css
.login-button {
  -webkit-appearance: none;
  appearance: none;
}

/* Visible ring for keyboard users */
.login-button:focus-visible {
  outline: 3px solid #1a73e8;
  outline-offset: 2px;
}

/* Fallback for older Safari without :focus-visible (before 15.4) */
@supports not selector(:focus-visible) {
  .login-button:focus {
    outline: 3px solid #1a73e8;
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }
}
```

Notes:
- Older Safari versions also ignored `border-radius` on outlines, so if you need a rounded ring there, `box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px #1a73e8;` alongside the outline is a common workaround.
- Make sure the ring colour has at least 3:1 contrast against the background around the button.

If it still disappears, paste the button's CSS (and any reset you use) and which Safari version, and I'll pin down which rule is winning.
